explanation:
The definition builds an instance of the library's `ClassFormation` structure on the units formation of the completion K_w. The `ClassFormation` structure asks for:
- H¹ vanishing on every layer;
- injective invariant maps whose image is the torsion subgroup of order equal to the layer's degree;
- the restriction law (invariants multiply by the relative degree), the inflation law (invariants are unchanged) and invariance under conjugation.

The invariant `realLayerInv` is defined by cases. It is 0 on a trivial layer, where H² is trivial anyway. On the unique quadratic layer it is `infiniteInvMap ∘ brInfl`, i.e. the archimedean invariant after inflation into the Brauer group. `realLayerInv_apply` shows it sends every nonzero class to 1/2, which is the standard normalization inv: Br(ℝ) ≅ ½ℤ/ℤ. All axioms are proved, so this is the standard archimedean class formation with the standard normalization.

It is a specific object: the class formation attached to a real place, whose content depends on K_w ≅ ℝ. So the restriction to real places is not narrowness; the complex case is handled separately in `infiniteClassFormationOfIsComplex`. Mathlib has no class formations, so there is no counterpart.
